  "The North Field": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — boreal river-grass plain. A broad wet-grass plain stretches beneath ribbon clouds and upward-falling rain. Slow shining channels appear, braid, and disappear through blue-green sedge; reed-seeds drift at dusk like suspended lanterns. The western grass grows pale where the Reaches breathe fog across it, the eastern bank lifts into the first folded terraces, and the southern waterways narrow toward the Threshold March and Stone Rise. Nothing resembles an Aerfálan river plain: water sometimes climbs a reed before falling into the sky, and every cloud has a faint reflection moving through the channels below.\n\nLiving Ecology: The Field is the northern circulatory bed. It receives melt-thread and condensed mist from the northwest, mineral seepage from the Stone Rise, and the Demi-Plane's north wind; it sends water, silt, seeds, and dissolved mana south. Rivergrass roots move with the channels instead of resisting them. Lantern geese carry luminous seed in their feathers, reed elk crop old growth, channel serpents keep shoals from stripping new roots, and blue silt-worms turn abandoned streambeds into fertile ground. A channel's disappearance is migration, not loss: its water continues underground until another region needs it.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 8–20): reed elk with translucent antlers; lantern geese whose bellies glow over water; ribbonfin shoals; sleek channel serpents; rain-swifts that swim upward through glittering showers; and small wind-sprites behaving as flocking, non-sapient creatures along the cloud bands.\n\nResources: rivergrass, luminous reed-seed, freshwater herbs, mana-rich silt, shed reed-elk velvet, and clear channel water collected without damming a living course.\n\nKnown Locations: Shining Channels and Northwind Plain. Seasonal ponds, migrating reed islands, submerged burrows, and temporary crossings shift with the Field and remain discoveries.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "The north wind is the Demi-Plane's breathing circulation. When the Mana Heart is steady, its pulse moves visibly through grass from south to north and back again through the channels. The Field is already choosing where water is needed: dry soil elsewhere causes one of its channels to sink and reappear near that region days later. Permanent diversion would starve more than this tile, so irrigation here must work with the moving water rather than pin it down.",
