  "The Fold": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — folded scrub-steppe. Grass, flowering heath, and low stone rise in immense pleats, with ridges repeating like paper caught halfway through an intricate fold. Valleys return to themselves, nearby slopes meet only after a long walk, and distant ground sometimes touches at the corner of the eye. Westward, the pleats flatten into North Field rivergrass; southward, moss thickens toward Stone Rise; eastward, silver scrub catches aurora spilled from the Veiled Edge. Its palette is sage, ink-blue, and flashes of copper foxfire running along creases at dusk.\n\nLiving Ecology: The Fold turns geometry into habitat. Warm and cool faces of the same ridge can occupy adjacent steps, letting northern herbs, dry scrub, moss, and boundary grass exchange pollen without one overwhelming the others. Fold-martens carry nuts through shortcuts; crease cicadas aerate the thin soil; loop hounds herd grazing stonebacks without ever exhausting them; and twin-root shrubs grow one crown from two distant root beds. Water travels by adjacency rather than slope, emerging in small basins far from the rain that filled them. The region functions as a living exchange corridor between four unlike biomes.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 15–28): fold-martens that disappear tail-first into narrow creases; loop hounds that return ahead of their own tracks; pleatback tortoises grazing two slopes at once; origami-wing cicadas; warped stone grazers; and sideways-drifting spatial wisps with the instincts of insects rather than people.\n\nResources: warped stone, spatial crystal, twin-site herbs, pleatback shell shed naturally, and foldglass formed where two slopes briefly touch.\n\nKnown Locations: Folding Ridges and Returning Valley. Impossible caves, folded pockets, shortcut burrows, and rain basins are learned by repeated travel, never fixed confidently on a conventional map.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "The warped terrain is a natural defense, but its deeper purpose is ecological: it prevents isolated biomes from becoming closed systems. Creatures that learn a fold carry spores, minerals, and seeds across the whole northern tier. Invaders without permission are returned in widening loops; native fauna and recognized travelers follow scent-lines of copper foxfire that remain invisible to strangers. Flattening the Fold would damage both the realm's defenses and its most efficient migration network.",
