  "The Mirror Mere": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — twilight wetland. Still pools, violet reed-beds, and gentle channels lie beneath a sky held at blue-gold twilight. Water glows softly and retains a reflection for several breaths after its subject moves away. Opaline grit arrives from the Veiled Edge; mineral streams descend from Stone Rise; overflow slips south into Dawn Stream. The western pools narrow into mossy rivulets, while the easternmost water reflects the boundary aurora even when clouds cover it.\n\nLiving Ecology: The Mere regulates both water and mana. Mirror-lotus absorbs emotional and magical turbulence, moonfin shoals graze the resulting glow, glass frogs keep insect swarms balanced, and long-legged reflection herons move nutrients between isolated pools. Reed roots filter mineral runoff; spirit mussels turn excess mana into pearls; night-blooming algae releases it slowly back into the channels. The surface's beauty is functional—calm water means the realm's circulation is balanced.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 10–22): moonfin fish, glass frogs, reflection herons, pearlback mussels, lantern newts, water-foxes with slick silver coats, and shapeless lightlings that school like fish beneath the surface without ever becoming people.\n\nResources: spirit pearls, luminous reeds, stable mana-water, mirror-lotus, opaline boundary grit, and naturally shed heron glass-feathers.\n\nKnown Locations: Twilight Pools and Reflection Basin. Hidden reed islands, submerged root-caves, migrating shoals, and brief reflection-paths emerge with water level and light.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "The Mere remembers impressions rather than truth. A pool can replay a creature's passage, weather, or intense spell, but never supplies motives, names, or facts it did not physically reflect. This prevents reflection magic from becoming omniscient. When the Mana Heart surges, the Mere stores the excess in pearls and lotus roots; during a lull, moonfin disturb those roots and release it downstream. Removing too many living pearls would reduce that buffering capacity.",
