  "The Quiet Shore": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — pale coast. Moon-white sand descends to a perfectly still horizon that behaves like water without breathing like a sea. Pale dune-grass flowers under starlight; saltgrass chimes once for each new wind and never repeats the same note. Northward the dunes receive cedar needles and luminous pollen from the Sanctuary, eastward the sand becomes Westreach loam, and southward the not-water condenses into mist that feeds the Low Reaches. Beneath the flat surface, fish-shaped lights pass as though swimming under glass.\n\nLiving Ecology: The Shore converts boundary stillness into water for the western realm. Night dew beads across the not-water, rolls inland through buried shell beds, and emerges fresh in marsh and meadow. Glass crabs sift dimensional residue from the tideline; moonplovers carry dune seed east; silent dune foxes bury pale driftwood beetles; and shellback skates graze light just below the surface. Nothing is washed ashore without entering the cycle—wood becomes fungal nursery, shell becomes sand, and unfamiliar matter is isolated in crystal until the realm accepts or rejects it.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 8–18): glass crabs, moonplovers, silent dune foxes, shellback skates beneath the not-water, pearl-bodied tide moths, and luminous fish-shapes that follow the false currents without crossing the boundary.\n\nResources: moon-sand, saltgrass chimes, shell crystal, pale driftwood, tide-moth silk, and boundary dew collected after it reaches the inland catch-basins.\n\nKnown Locations: Stillwater Shore and Moonwhite Strand. Tidal hollows, shell nurseries, stranded curios, and impossible reflections remain transient discoveries.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "The western boundary is neither unfinished nor empty; it is a condenser. A future sea or desert would change its expression, but the current coast already supports the Low Reaches and western meadows through underground dewflow. The not-water reflects possibilities that approached the realm without entering it. Those images are not prophecies and carry no knowledge, only shape and motion. The Chamberlain's surveys missed the freshwater cycle because every drop moves below the sand.",
