  "The Westreach": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — open mixed grassland. Rolling silver-green pasture, red sorrel, cowslip, and scattered lantern saplings spread between the Quiet Shore, First Meadow, Heartgate Basin, and Hollow Vale. Dew gathers at dawn in humming beads of blue-violet foxfire. Faint road-lines show where movement has encouraged tougher grass, yet flowering verges and young groves keep the landscape alive. The future Market Square, Guesthouse, and Training Yard remain measured possibilities within a functioning meadow-woodland mosaic, not scars cut into emptiness.\n\nLiving Ecology: Westreach is the realm's adaptable edge habitat. Meadow pollinators, shore birds, garden foxes, and vale burrowers overlap here; young lantern trees store excess dawn-light and release it after dusk. Dewback tortoises carry seed and fungus between groves, grass mice aerate future building ground, and small treants—plantlike creatures rather than residents—move saplings out of flood paths. The responsive soil settles around foundations while preserving watercourses, root lanes, and animal crossings.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 1–10): dewback tortoises, meadow cattle, dawn-moths, grass mice, garden foxes, young treant-creatures, jewel-wing starlings, and territorial but non-sapient spirit beasts that mark good grazing ground with harmless light.\n\nResources: lantern-tree sap, foxfire dew beads, common herbs, responsibly taken young timber, fertile soil from planned excavation, and sorrel seed.\n\nKnown Locations: Future Market Square, Guesthouse Site, and Training Yard Site. Natural wells, small groves, fox paths, and seasonal flowerbanks remain part of the living ground around future development.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "Westreach soil responds quickly because one of the Mana Heart's broad western currents passes beneath it, but it does not obey construction blindly. Stakes placed across a root corridor lean overnight; planned walls over a burrow line gather luminous moss; a well appears wherever a building would otherwise interrupt drainage. Reading these responses lets development grow with the ecosystem without changing the established settlement mechanics or the three marked sites.",
