  "The Dawn Barrow": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — stream-fed river meadow. Clear water threads through wet-grass flats, redstone shelves, and wildflower islands under the soft light of perpetual morning. It descends from Stone Rise and Mirror Mere, crosses the meadow in luminous braids, then sends one branch west toward Heartgate gardens and another beneath the grass toward the Deep Expanse and Southroll. Red stone glows coral, rose, and foxfire-gold beneath the current. The landscape remains open and meadowlike while feeling unmistakably otherworldly.\n\nLiving Ecology: Dawn Stream is the realm's visible waterway and migration route. Brook foxes cache shells along the banks; redscale lizards warm themselves on exposed stones; ribbonfin carry Mirror Mere algae downstream; and mist swallows harvest insects at dawn. Floating flower islands transport pollen, frog eggs, and fungal spores between pools. Floodwater never arrives as an ordinary destructive surge: it rises in luminous steps across redstone shelves, replenishes the flats, then drains cleanly into underground prairie channels.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 4–14): brook foxes, redscale lizards, ribbonfin shoals, meadow deer, mist swallows, jewel frogs, small river sprites with animal instincts, and long-legged dawn cranes that follow the stream's newest bends.\n\nResources: redstone, clear mana-water, river herbs, wetland flowers, ribbonfin scales shed at spawning beds, and floating-island root fiber.\n\nKnown Locations: Dawn Stream and Redstone Crossing. New bends, flower islands, undercut grottoes, and brief waterfalls are found by following the living water.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "The stream is the Demi-Plane's first autonomous hydrological link and now joins multiple regions without replacing their identities. Its glow records what it carries: violet after Mirror Mere stores a mana surge, silver after Stone Rise runoff, warm gold when Heartgate orchards are flowering. Damming it would silence those signals and starve downstream prairie roots. Redstone Crossing formed where the water itself concentrated stable stones around a deep root junction.",
