  "The Easthold": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — sunlit dry-grass upland. Hard warm ground, amber-gold grass, and low ember shrubs lie beneath late-afternoon light with no visible sun. Dust hangs like slow sparks. Mirror Mere's twilight cools the northern edge, Dawn Barrow's stream greens the west, Southroll's hills soften the southern boundary, and the east tightens into the dark clefts of Rift March. Mana vents breathe ribbons of heat through stone without smoke, making the upland feel forged rather than scorched.\n\nLiving Ecology: Easthold is the realm's heat exchange. Sunscale lizards and gilt scarabs absorb excess warmth by day and carry it into burrows at night; ember shrubs store mana in waxy seedpods; thermal hawks keep the dryland grazers moving; and vent fungi convert mineral heat into edible golden caps. Their cycle prevents concentrated mana from becoming wildfire. Seeds sent west germinate along Dawn Stream, while heated updrafts carry Southroll pollen north toward the Mirror Mere's cooler margins.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 10–22): sun hawks, sunscale lizards, gilt scarabs, amber-backed foxes, dryland antelope, vent moths, and mana-charged predators adapted to hunt by heat shimmer rather than unexplained magic.\n\nResources: mana-stone, heat crystal, golden grass, ember-shrub wax, vent fungus, and dryland herbs harvested after their seedpods cool.\n\nKnown Locations: Eastern Expansion Site and Golden Upland. Mana vents, half-formed workshop shapes, heat hollows, and mineral nests remain discoverable features rather than finished facilities.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "The warmth is excess mana bled safely from Heartgate's circulation through Stone Rise bedrock. The ecosystem is part of that safety valve: gilt scarabs bury hot crystal dust, vent fungi consume it, and plant roots distribute the moderated heat. The established Expansion Site still offers power for a forge, workshop, or other development, but the strongest foundations follow cool corridors left by the scarabs rather than sealing every vent.",
