  "The Low Reaches": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — shifting marshy lowland. Soft black ground, luminous pools, reed islets, and rain-silver hollows form and reform beneath drifting green-violet will-o'-wisps. The north receives buried dewflow from the Quiet Shore and runoff from Westreach; Hollow Vale water enters from the east; southward, the marsh thickens into Dim March moss and Grey Boundary fog. Pools trade places overnight, but the biome remains a coherent wetland rather than random terrain.\n\nLiving Ecology: The Reaches are the western filter and nursery. Glow-reeds draw impurities from water, dream-salamanders carry their eggs between migrating pools, chorus frogs regulate insects, mud-foxes turn peat, and luminous waterplants release stored light after dusk. Spirit peat forms from shed reed and moss instead of dead stagnation. Shifting ground lets the marsh rest used channels and open fresh ones, preventing mana from accumulating dangerously in a single pool.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 8–18): dream-salamanders, chorus frogs, mud-foxes, mirror gnats, bog turtles with mossy shells, reed-stalking mire cats, and pale will-o'-wisps behaving as pollinating swamp creatures rather than speaking guides.\n\nResources: glow-reed, spirit peat, luminous waterplant, marsh herbs, dream-salamander shed-skin, and clear water taken only after it passes through a living reed bed.\n\nKnown Locations: The Shifting Pools and Rain Hollow. Bog grottoes, temporary nesting islets, surfaced curios, and slow-moving reed gardens remain discoveries.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "The instability is ecological motion powered by surplus potential, not ground waiting to be forced still. Focused intention can stabilize a chosen site for construction, but freezing the whole region would destroy its filtration cycle. Pool color identifies what the marsh is processing: green for plant-rich runoff, blue for excess mana, pearl for boundary dew, and violet for organic decay. The Dim March receives only what the Reaches have rendered inert and preservable.",
