  "The Deep Expanse": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — vast prairie plain. Blue-green grass rolls beneath an immense clear sky, humming in long chords when the wind crosses it. The Threshold and Heartgate root-streams enter from the north, Hollow Vale terraces step down at the west, Southroll gold warms the east, and the open southern grasses continue naturally into the Open Horizon. Far-off herds shimmer like mirages, and at night the grass tips reproduce the sky as millions of low cobalt lights.\n\nLiving Ecology: The Expanse is the realm's largest nutrient and mana reservoir. Deep roots store water from Dawn Barrow and spread it across the southern tier; mana amber forms where old roots crystallize harmless surplus. Prairie drakes prune tall grass with low wingbeats, glass-antler herds migrate in wide circles, burrowing thunder-moles aerate deep soil, and seed foxes cache windseed along the routes. Predators follow herds, scavengers clear remains, and amber fungus returns concentrated mana to new growth.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 10–22): prairie drakes, glass-antler herds, seed foxes, thunder-moles, long-wing kites, amber-backed scavengers, and immense gentle grasswhales whose backs barely rise above the tallest waves of grass.\n\nResources: mana amber, tallgrass herbs, windseed, shed drake membrane, naturally cast antler glass, and amber fungus gathered after a root-cycle completes.\n\nKnown Locations: Great Prairie and Mana-Density Survey Point. Herd routes, root caverns, amber fields, predator ranges, and ancient-seeming stone circles remain wilderness discoveries.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "The deep reserve grows because every adjacent region sends something here: silt, water, pollen, heat, or decayed root. It is not limitless. The Survey Point measures both magical density and ecological pressure; rising mana paired with falling herd movement signals dangerous stagnation. A future city, forest, or mountain range can be developed here under the existing system, but doing so redirects migration and root flow, creating real consequences rather than empty-space construction.",
