  "The Southroll": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — golden hill country. Rounded grass hills hold perpetual late-afternoon light in honey, apricot, and rose-gold. Low herbs perfume warm soil; fireflies move in disciplined spirals around hill crests. Easthold's dry grass blends along the northeast, Deep Expanse prairie enters from the west, Rift March stone breaks the east, and southern hills gradually lose color into the Fade. The region feels restful without becoming inert.\n\nLiving Ecology: Southroll stores gentle heat and releases it through food, pollen, and moving air. Warmroot draws warmth into edible rhizomes; sunmoths pollinate golden herbs; hill foxes bury surplus roots; drowsy grazing beasts keep grass short; and firefly spirals distribute luminous spores along the wind. Hollow groves condense cool water overnight, feeding both Rift March cave mouths and Fade boundary herbs. The cycle makes the region a refuge during wider instability without freezing it in permanent calm.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 3–12): hill foxes, sunmoths, honeybees with glass wings, warm-feather quail, drowsy round-backed grazers, disciplined firefly clouds, and small golden drakes that surf the lee of each hill.\n\nResources: warmroot, golden herbs, firefly dust naturally shed from finished spirals, glasswing honey, sweet grass, and sunmoth silk.\n\nKnown Locations: Sunset Hills and Golden Walk. Picnic groves, cool springs, flower slopes, and animal dens emerge naturally without becoming settlements.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "The golden light is low-state mana sustained by the living hill cycle. If other systems fail, Southroll keeps seeds warm, animals fed, and water moving for several days. Its stability depends on motion: fireflies must spiral, grazers must migrate, and warmroot must be eaten and replanted. Removing all harvest limits would turn the supposed lifeboat into a sterile bright field.",
