  "The Rift March": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — broken marchland. Golden grass fractures into dark ridges and deep clefts lit from below by cold cobalt fire. Echoes return late by a heartbeat; stone faces show mineral veins like frozen lightning. Southroll herbs cling to western shelves, Easthold heat enters from the north, the Fade softens the southern rim, and the far eastern fissures lean toward the Last Edge's fixed silence. The landscape stays harsh, dark, and dungeon-suited while participating in the wider realm.\n\nLiving Ecology: Life here feeds on pressure, mineral, and echo. Rift crawlers scrape cave fungus from sheer walls; bluejaw drakes hunt them along thermal shafts; echo bats map moving stone; and darkroot vines bridge cracks long enough for seed and small animals to cross. Cold-light fungus converts blue mana seepage into food, while stone lichens seal minor fractures. Predation is real and dangerous, but every creature occupies a material cycle instead of existing solely to attack visitors.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 18–30): rift crawlers, bluejaw cave drakes, echo bats, darkstone mantises, cleft foxes, blue-flame lures with animal instincts, and larger stone predators camouflaged against the eastern walls.\n\nResources: rift crystal, darkstone, blue mana ore, cold-light fungus, echo-bat membrane shed in roosts, and darkroot fiber.\n\nKnown Locations: Eastern Clefts and Rift Overlook. Cave systems, nests, mineral chambers, strange ruins, and any future dungeon entrance remain unmarked discoveries within the established region.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "The clefts arose partly from fears embedded in the realm's latent memory, but the ecosystem has domesticated that tension without making the ground safe. Cobalt fungus drinks the unstable mana those fears condensed; predators spread its spores; rift crystal traps what remains. The deepest fissures approach the boundary and could support a dungeon through the existing development logic, but they do not currently conceal an invented civilization.",
