  "The Dim March": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — hushed moss-hollow. Pale moss, low silver fungus, and soft hollows lie beneath dim, sourceless light. Footsteps fade before they land, water moves without trickle, and even wind crosses as a pressure rather than a sound. The Low Reaches gradually trade reed and peat for moss at the north; Grey Boundary fog enters from the east; the western rim softens into quiet blankness. Candle Hollow holds the brightest pool of resting light.\n\nLiving Ecology: The March is the realm's decomposer bed and preservation vault. Stillmoss separates decay from spoilage: useful material remains stable while mold, beetles, and thread-fungi dismantle what must return to soil. Hush moths pollinate in silence, moss voles turn old peat, and sleeping shade-beasts regulate deeper burrowers when awakened by excess noise. Water filtered by the Low Reaches rests here before fog carries its minerals toward the Grey Boundary.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 18–28): hush moths, moss voles, pale velvet foxes, silent snails, shade-beasts sleeping beneath deep banks, and thread-thin fungus creatures that recoil from sound and feed on spent enchantment residue.\n\nResources: stillmoss peat, preservation moss, muted spirit-stone, silence-thread fungus, shed hush-moth velvet, and inert alchemical residue reclaimed from the moss.\n\nKnown Locations: Candle Hollow. Storage hollows, old mana pockets, silent nests, and the Chamberlain's concealed Emergency Cache remain deliberately unmarked.",
    "factions": [],
