  "The Grey Boundary": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — fog-wall moor. Cool heather, silver sedge, black pools, and anchor-stone cairns stretch before a horizon-to-horizon wall of grey fog. Shapes flicker within it too briefly to become recognizable. Dim March moss enters from the west, Hollow Vale runoff crosses the northern moor, and Open Horizon grass begins eastward. The fog absorbs direction but not color: foxfire along the ground burns a deep wine-red here, the one warm note in the grey.\n\nLiving Ecology: This is the realm's stabilizing bog and fog catch. Boundary moss curls toward spatial stress, moor hares graze heather, grey moths carry spores along the fixed edge, and anchor beetles pack dense stone grit into their burrows. Their colonies create thousands of tiny braces across the moor. Water arriving from the Vale and March condenses into black pools, loses unstable mana through fog reeds, then seeps east beneath the Open Horizon.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 22–30): moor hares, grey moths, anchor beetles, long-legged fog deer, pool eels, wine-flame foxlets, and rare silent anchor guardians already established by the realm's defenses.\n\nResources: anchor-stone, fog crystal, boundary moss, condensed dimensional mist, fog-reed pith, and discarded anchor-beetle casing.\n\nKnown Locations: The Grey Fog Wall and Anchor Cairn. Forgotten cairns, black pools, fog pockets, and half-lost objects remain exploratory discoveries.",
    "factions": [],
    "hiddenInfo": "The southern fog fixes the Demi-Plane's orientation relative to the mortal world. The visible wall is only the largest brace; roots, beetle-packed burrows, cairns, and anchor-stone seams distribute that stability across the moor. The ecosystem reacts to drift hours before the Gate becomes unreliable: moths land, pools flatten, and every boundary-moss curl points southeast. The Chamberlain senses the border must remain stable but has not yet read the full living warning system.",
