  "The Fade": {
    "basicInfo": "Biome — gentle hills dissolving into featureless mist. Soft hills lose sound first, then color, then certainty as they descend into pearl-grey mist. Southroll gold warms the northwestern slopes, Rift March stone darkens the northeast, Open Horizon grasses enter from the west, and Last Edge stillness presses from the east. Cairns mark where reliable ground ends. Boundary flowers bloom in gradients—gold, lilac, white, then transparent—making the transition visible without changing the map's hill-fen character.\n\nLiving Ecology: Fade life specializes in partial existence. Mistlings graze condensation, echo hares follow a heartbeat behind their bodies, translucent root-vines hold loose hills together, and pearl moths pollinate flowers that disappear after seeding. Seeds carried out of the mist become ordinary solid plants in Southroll or Open Horizon; organic matter carried inward returns as mist nutrients. This two-way exchange lets the unfinished boundary support life without pretending it is fully formed land.\n\nWildlife & Encounters (levels 20–30): mistlings, echo hares, pearl moths, pale walkers with the behavior of shy grazing beasts, translucent foxes, and rare fade-beasts that consume unstable outlines before those outlines harden into hazardous terrain.\n\nResources: fadeglass, mist pearls, soundless stone, boundary herbs, pearl-moth silk, and transparent seed husks collected on the solid side of the Cairn Line.\n\nKnown Locations: Cairn Line and The Dissolving Hills. Momentary paths, vanishing groves, temporary structures, and overlapping versions of the same slope remain discoveries and never fixed settlements.",
